          en: 'The ability to create and maintain study plans is directly transferable to the professional environment. Planning sprints, estimating tasks, managing time, and prioritizing activities are variations of the same skill set. The professional who demonstrates self-management capability in learning demonstrates self-management capability at work. This is particularly relevant for remote work, where autonomy and discipline are prerequisites.\n\nIn interviews, describing your study process clearly — what you studied, how you organized it, how much time you dedicated, what you produced — demonstrates professional maturity beyond technical knowledge. It is not about impressing with hours of dedication, but about showing that you approach learning with the same seriousness you would approach a professional project.',
